  "answer_core": "Given your constraints and the seven‑day clock, treat this as a sequencing problem rather than choosing among A, B, and C in a vacuum.\n\n1. Don’t stay. The plateau and lack of a path to staff aren’t going to resolve, and staying is effectively choosing continued stagnation.\n\n2. Run the spouse conversation immediately. Option B is only viable with explicit joint buy‑in since it changes income, schedule, and risk exposure. Use that conversation to establish boundaries (expected hours, weekend protection, financial guardrails) and confirm whether this level of volatility is acceptable right now.\n\n3. In parallel, perform fast due diligence on Option B: runway, burn, funding cadence, CEO track record, hiring plan, equity structure, dilution model, and what “founding engineer” concretely means. Also ask for a deadline extension if needed.\n\n4. If both spouse‑alignment and due‑diligence pass, pursue Option B. It resolves your desire to build, offers meaningful ownership, and avoids another two years of delay.\n\n5. If either fails, take Option A as a two‑year bridge. It gives you stability, modest comp lift, and space to rethink your long‑term direction without the structural ceiling of your current job.\n\nThis sequence gets you to a decision within the deadline while aligning family constraints, resolving the plateau, and avoiding premature commitment to a startup without validation.",
